{"ad infinitum":{"id":"6eee9f32-883a-4ba4-ad10-62187f313efc","word":"ad infinitum","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"endlessly","word":"ad infinitum"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"endlessly","word":"infinitamente"}],"pos":"adv","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The idea of eternal return is a mysterious one, and Nietzsche has often perplexed other philosophers with it: to think that everything recurs as we once experienced it, and that the recurrence itself recurs ad infinitum!","ref":"1984, Milan Kundera, part I (Lightness and Weight), chapter I, in Michael Henry Heim, transl., The Unbearable Lightness of Being, London: Faber and Faber, translation of Nesnesitelná lehkost bytí, published 1985, →ISBN, page 3:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[207,219]],"id":"227cc8f9-924e-40fb-916a-43c93653e1a2"}],"links":[["Endlessly","endlessly"],["for ever","for ever"],["never-endingly","never-endingly"]],"categories":["English adverbs","English degree adverbs","English entries with incorrect language header","English learned borrowings from Latin","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English terms borrowed from Latin","English terms derived from Latin","English terms with quotations","English unadapted borrowings from Latin","English uncomparable adverbs","Entries with translation boxes","Mandarin terms with non-redundant manual transliterations","Mandarin terms with redundant transliterations","Pages with 6 entries","Pages with entries","Rhymes:English/aɪtəm","Terms with Bulgarian translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Hindi translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Latin translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Norwegian translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Serbo-Croatian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Tagalog translations","Terms with Vietnamese translations","en:Infinity"],"glosses":["Endlessly; for ever; never-endingly."],"tags":["not-comparable"],"id":"3a793055-3690-47fa-8fcd-525874e2645c"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"adieu":{"id":"0048e018-c9b1-4adc-ad72-ed6d6fa8a4f6","word":"adieu","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a farewell","tags":["masculine"],"word":"adeus"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a farewell","tags":["feminine"],"word":"despedida"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"We bid our final adieus to our family, then boarded the ship, bound for America.","bold_text_offsets":[[17,23]],"type":"example","id":"b98ea8de-9bfb-438f-a8a1-326e2fd3241d"},{"text":"As Noyes bade me adieu and rode off northward in his car I began to walk slowly toward the house.","ref":"1931, H. P. Lovecraft, chapter 6, in The Whisperer in Darkness:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[17,22]],"id":"a31cf08d-8221-4aaa-aa26-239d61d57e18"},{"text":"Yes, the tide will surely turn, and meanwhile may one who is proud to call himself a partisan, invite whomever may feel disposed to bid the \"T14s\" adieux, to pause before giving them valediction and accord to them the respect that is assuredly their due.","ref":"1952 January, Henry Maxwell, “Farewell to the \"T14s\"”, in Railway Magazine, page 57:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[147,153]],"id":"12a922e7-f479-4015-b485-fb4976daad0e"},{"text":"At the of his so remote, so near, 1884 summer Van, before leaving Ardis, was to make a visit of adieu to Ada's larvarium.","ref":"1969, Vladimir Nabokov, Ada or Ardor: A Family Chronicle:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[96,101]],"id":"33e17ef1-b37d-4102-a504-803f92c59eed"}],"links":[["farewell","farewell"],["goodbye","goodbye"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["A farewell, a goodbye; especially a fond farewell, or a lasting or permanent farewell."],"id":"f48e1500-3632-451c-a5be-ea7e70a9ef8d"}],"related":[{"word":"à deux"},{"word":"ado"}],"synonyms":[]},"adipocere":{"id":"b3c76af1-870c-4bc9-a27e-3b876cccb853","word":"adipocere","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"waxy substance that forms on dead animal tissues","tags":["feminine"],"word":"adipocera"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"In some cases, however, brains outlast all other soft tissues and remain intact for hundreds or thousands of years. […] [Alexandra] Morton-Hayward's experiments suggest the brains endure through a process called molecular cross-linking, in which brain protein remnants and degraded lipids form a spongy polymer. […] The mechanism appears distinct from how some other bodily tissues are preserved after turning to adipocere, or \"grave wax,\" which forms when body fats transform into a tallow-colored soaplike substance. \"Adipocere forms in adipose tissue — that's buttocks, arms, cheeks,\" says Sonia O'Connor, an archaeologist and pioneering researcher of ancient brains at the University of Bradford in England. \"There is no adipose tissue in the brain. It's the wrong chemistry.\"","ref":"2025, “Advances: Biology: Brain Endurance. Misfolded proteins may preserve brains for millennia”, in Scientific American, volume 332, number 2 (February), pages 10-12:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[413,422],[520,529]],"id":"bf31ff56-e727-435e-a929-1ca7814f18ba"}],"links":[["brown","brown"],["fatty","fatty"],["waxy","waxy"],["substance","substance"],["fatty acid","fatty acid"],["dead","dead"],["animal","animal"],["tissue","tissue"],["hydrolysis","hydrolysis"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"grave wax"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms derived from Latin","English terms prefixed with adipo-","English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Indonesian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","en:Medicine"],"glosses":["A brown, fatty, waxy substance (fatty acids) that forms on dead animal tissues in response to hydrolysis."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"1092b252-8de3-4ead-8b5c-00b56f5a50dd"}],"related":[{"word":"adipocerate"},{"word":"adipoceration"},{"word":"adipoceriform"},{"word":"adipocerous"}],"synonyms":[]},"adiabatic":{"id":"61d4fb7c-b74b-4cb2-95a7-d0c8d3b45a84","word":"adiabatic","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"occurring without gain or loss of heat","tags":["masculine"],"word":"adiabático"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The line drawn on the indicator diagram in the latter case has been named by Professor Rankine an Adiabatic line, because it is defined by the condition that heat is not allowed to pass through (διαβαίνειν) the vessel which confines the substance.","ref":"1871, James Clerk Maxwell, Theory of Heat, page 129:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[98,107]],"id":"03c5c337-50e2-457f-801e-534092e48363"},{"text":"Talk of dynamic compression and adiabatic gradients didn't carry as much weight as the certainty of its conscious intent.","ref":"2006, Thomas Pynchon, Against the Day, Vintage 2007, page 737:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[32,41]],"id":"72832401-5a8d-4053-880b-40072c741f78"},{"text":"New 2.4 GWh adiabatic compressed air energy storage (CAES) plant now operational in in ^([sic]) Jiangsu province.","ref":"2026 January 27, Lynas, Matthew, “World’s largest compressed-air storage plant switched on in China”, in pv magazine:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[12,21]],"id":"4a4b4cf8-e990-43ce-8998-cb00bb4d4b5c"}],"topics":["natural-sciences","physical-sciences","physics","thermodynamics"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Physics","en:Thermodynamics"],"links":[["physics","physics"],["thermodynamics","thermodynamics"],["process","process"],["gain","gain"],["loss","loss"],["heat","heat"],["entropy","entropy"],["quasistatic","quasistatic"]],"antonyms":[{"word":"diabatic"}],"raw_glosses":["(physics, thermodynamics, of a process) Without gain or loss of heat (and thus with no change in entropy, in the quasistatic approximation)."],"glosses":["Without gain or loss of heat (and thus with no change in entropy, in the quasistatic approximation)."],"tags":["not-comparable"],"raw_tags":["of a process"],"id":"c5831441-a2cc-479a-897c-3bfe899d906a"},{"examples":[{"text":"In this section we examine the limiting cases when T is very small (sudden change) and very large (adiabatic change).","ref":"1961, Albert Messiah, Quantum Mechanics, volume II, page 740:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[99,108]],"id":"b78f3ee1-f5c0-4101-aca3-d66e935a8120"}],"topics":["natural-sciences","physical-sciences","physics"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned","en:Physics","en:Quantum mechanics"],"links":[["physics","physics"],["quantum mechanics","quantum mechanics"],["Hamiltonian","Hamiltonian"]],"antonyms":[{"word":"nonadiabatic"}],"raw_glosses":["(physics, quantum mechanics, of a process) Involving the slow change of the Hamiltonian of a system from its initial value to a final value."],"qualifier":"quantum mechanics","glosses":["Involving the slow change of the Hamiltonian of a system from its initial value to a final value."],"tags":["not-comparable"],"raw_tags":["of a process"],"id":"5e5321c1-e0ca-4eb2-918e-11ac84ed2d